course: dictionary
lesson: 14833
title: Racetrack
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A racetrack is a place where things race round and round. It can be a loop for horses, cars, bikes or runners. When you read the word, look for the clues around it: words like race, lap, crowd or cars tell you what kind of place it is.
explain: «The runners lined up at the start of the racetrack.» Runners, start and lined up all point to a race, so a racetrack must be where the race happens. Try your meaning in the sentence: "The runners lined up at the start of a place where races happen." It still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
explain: Maths can use racetracks too. «One lap of the racetrack is 400 metres.» If one lap is 400 metres, then five laps are five lots of 400, which is 2,000 metres. A racetrack loop helps you practise multiplying.
fact: A racetrack is a track, usually an oval or loop, where horses, cars, bikes or runners race.
fact: A racetrack can also be a place where horse races are held, with stands for the crowd. This is also called a racecourse.
fact: A lap is one trip all the way round the racetrack.
fact: «Cars zoomed round the racetrack all afternoon.» Zoomed tells you the cars were fast.
fact: A racetrack can be used in maths: if one lap is 400 metres, then 5 laps are 2,000 metres.
exercise: «Cars zoomed round the racetrack all afternoon.» What is a racetrack? | A shop that sells tyres | A road where buses park | A kind of sandwich | *A track where cars, horses or runners race
exercise: «One lap of the racetrack is 400 metres.» How far are five laps? | *2,000 metres | 40 metres | 200 metres | 4,500 metres
exercise: Which word means a place where horse races are held, also called a racecourse? | pavement | library | *racetrack | garden
test: «We watched the horses gallop round the racetrack.» Where did the horses gallop? | *On a track built for horse races | Up a tree | In a stable, resting | In the sea
test: Jo ran three laps of the racetrack. What is a lap? | A kind of race car | A fence round the crowd | *One trip all the way round the track | A wooden bench by the track
exam: «The crowd cheered from the stands as the cars roared past the racetrack.» What are the stands? | Ocean waves | Puddles of rain | *Seats where the crowd watches | Kitchens for the cooks
